scene{
    The unaided eye separates two things about a tenth of a millimetre apart. Almost nothing that biology calls a cell is that large, which is why the claim that living material is made of cells could not be made — let alone tested — until someone had ground a lens good enough.

    The table sets the sizes against the instruments. Read down the first column and the scale falls by five powers of ten between a human egg cell and the width of a DNA molecule; read across and each instrument gives out somewhere along that fall.

    definition{
        The smallest separation at which two points are still seen as two rather than one. It is a property of the instrument and the light it uses, and no amount of enlargement improves it.
    }(
        term: Resolution
    )

    The human egg is the exception that makes the point. At about $0.12\text{ mm}$ it is the one human cell a person can see without help, and it is visible because it is roughly a hundred times wider than the red blood cells in the same body.
}[
    table{
        Object                  | Approximate size      | Unaided eye                     | Light microscope                | Electron microscope
        Human egg cell          | $120\ \mu\text{m}$    | !icon(name: tick; colour: green) | !icon(name: tick; colour: green) | !icon(name: tick; colour: green)
        Red blood cell          | $8\ \mu\text{m}$      | !icon(name: cross; colour: red) | !icon(name: tick; colour: green) | !icon(name: tick; colour: green)
        *E. coli* bacterium     | $2\ \mu\text{m}$      | !icon(name: cross; colour: red) | !icon(name: tick; colour: green) | !icon(name: tick; colour: green)
        Influenza virus         | $100\text{ nm}$       | !icon(name: cross; colour: red) | !icon(name: cross; colour: red) | !icon(name: tick; colour: green)
        Ribosome                | $25\text{ nm}$        | !icon(name: cross; colour: red) | !icon(name: cross; colour: red) | !icon(name: tick; colour: green)
        DNA double helix, width | $2\text{ nm}$         | !icon(name: cross; colour: red) | !icon(name: cross; colour: red) | !icon(name: tick; colour: green)
    }(
        title: What each instrument can separate
        highlight rows: 2
        highlight colour: orange
    )
]

scene{
    # Magnification enlarges; resolution decides whether that helps

    Magnification is a ratio and nothing more: the image divided by the object. A light microscope with a $\times 40$ objective and a $\times 10$ eyepiece magnifies $\times 400$, and the two numbers multiply because the eyepiece enlarges the objective's image rather than the specimen.

    definition{
        The ratio of the size of an image to the size of the object that produced it. On a compound microscope it is the objective's power multiplied by the eyepiece's.
    }(
        term: Magnification
    )

    Nothing in that ratio promises detail. Photograph a light-microscope image and enlarge the print by a further ten times and the magnification is genuinely ten times greater, while the number of distinguishable features is exactly what it was.

    ===

    The reason there is a floor is that light diffracts. Two points closer together than about half a wavelength send out overlapping waves, and the objective receives one blur where the specimen held two objects. Ernst Abbe's expression for the smallest separation still resolved is $d = \lambda / (2\,\text{NA})$, where $\lambda$ is the wavelength and the numerical aperture $\text{NA}$ measures how wide a cone of light the lens collects.

    The canvas plots that expression for green light at $\lambda = 550\text{ nm}$. Increasing the aperture buys a great deal at first and almost nothing at the end: the best oil-immersion objectives reach $\text{NA} = 1.4$, giving {{abbe floor}} nanometres — the $0.2\ \mu\text{m}$ that every account of the light microscope quotes as its limit.

    An electron beam has a wavelength thousands of times shorter, which is the whole of the reason electron microscopes resolve to about $0.1\text{ nm}$. They are not better-built light microscopes; they are the same physics run with a shorter wave.
}[
    canvas.graph{
        curve(f: 275 / x; domain: [0.25, 1.5]; colour: blue; label: $d = \lambda / (2\,\text{NA})$)
        point(x: 0.65; y: 423; colour: orange; label: a good dry objective)

        cue{
            axis.label(y: 196; label: $0.2\ \mu\text{m}$; line; colour: grey)
            point(x: 1.4; y: 196; colour: orange; label: oil immersion)
        }(
            in: 1
        )
    }(
        x axis label: numerical aperture
        y axis label: smallest resolvable separation (nm)
        x domain: [0.2, 1.6]
        y domain: [0, 1200]
    )
]

scene{
    # The three statements, and what each rests on

    Cell theory is usually given as three claims, and they were not established together. Each waited on a different observation, and the third waited nearly two centuries after the first.

    definition{
        A theory in three parts: that all living things are made of one or more cells, that the cell is the smallest unit that can be called alive, and that every cell arises from a pre-existing cell by division.
    }(
        term: Cell theory
    )

    The three statements were not arrived at together: the first two were assembled from a century and a half of looking !cite:#hooke1665() !cite:#schleiden1838() !cite:#schwann1839(), and the third was added later !cite:#virchow1858(). The third is the one that does real work. The first two describe what is there; the third forbids something — it says that no cell has ever appeared in any way except from another cell, which is a claim strong enough to be wrong and therefore worth testing.

    note{
        The theory is stated for cells, and viruses are the standing awkwardness. A virus has genetic material and a protein coat, replicates, and evolves, but carries no machinery to do any of it and so does nothing at all outside a host cell. The usual resolution is to say that the theory is about living things and that a virus is not one — which is a decision about the word rather than a discovery about the virus.
    }
}[
    table{
        The claim                                        | The observation behind it                                                                                 | Who, and when
        All organisms are composed of one or more cells  | Compartments seen first in cork, then in every plant and animal tissue anyone examined                     | Hooke 1665; Schleiden 1838; Schwann 1839
        The cell is the basic unit of life               | Nothing smaller taken from a cell carries on any of the activities the whole cell performs                 | Schleiden and Schwann, 1838–39
        Every cell comes from a pre-existing cell        | Cells were repeatedly watched dividing, and never watched forming out of anything that was not already a cell | Virchow, 1855
    }(
        title: The three statements of cell theory
    )
]

scene{
    ## The experiment the third statement had to survive

    If cells only ever come from cells, then a sterile broth sealed away from existing cells must stay sterile forever. The rival position — that life arises spontaneously from non-living material given air and nutrients — makes the opposite prediction, and it had been the majority view for a very long time.

    Louis Pasteur's flasks settled it !cite:#pasteur1861() in a way that is worth following closely, because the design does one difficult thing. Boiling the broth kills whatever is in it, but sealing the flask afterwards also excludes air, and a defender of spontaneous generation could always say that the air was what the process needed.

    ===

    The swan neck admits air and stops dust. The bend is long and low, so airborne particles settle in it and never reach the liquid; the broth stays clear for months. Snap the neck off and the same flask, with the same broth and the same air, clouds within days.

    Nothing changed except whether pre-existing cells could get in. That is the third statement of cell theory, tested against the one alternative anyone was offering.

    task{
        A student examines a specimen at $\times 400$ and can just distinguish two structures. They photograph the image and enlarge the photograph five times. Have they resolved anything new, and what has changed?
    }[
        Nothing new. The enlargement raises the magnification to an effective $\times 2000$ but the resolution was fixed the moment the light passed through the objective — around $0.2\ \mu\text{m}$ for a light microscope. Structures closer together than that were merged into one blur before the photograph was taken, and enlarging the blur makes it a larger blur.
    ](
        title: Your turn
    )
}[
    canvas.diagram{
        box#boil{
            Broth boiled in the flask
            anything already living in it is killed
        }
        box#neck{
            Left open through a long swan neck
            air passes freely; dust settles in the bend
        }
        box#clear:terminal{No growth, month after month}
        arrow(from: #boil; to: #neck)
        arrow(from: #neck; to: #clear)

        cue.draw{
            box#break{
                The neck is snapped off
                same broth, same air, dust now reaches the liquid
            }
            box#cloudy:terminal{Growth within days}
            arrow(from: #neck; to: #break)
            arrow(from: #break; to: #cloudy)
        }(
            in: 1
        )
    }(
        flow: down
        title: Pasteur's swan-neck flasks, 1859–61
    )
]
